      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
As discussed, your house has some excess air leakage that is coming from a few key areas:

** The co-vented chimney for the current furnace and hot water tank (that would be eliminated with the recommended furnace and hot water tank upgrades)

** The chimney for the current wood stove is in very bad shape and actually has a hole rotted through in the elbow behind the wood stove. This wood stove &amp; chimney should be completely replaced with a new high efficiency unit (see further details in my Additional Comments section)

** Along the bottom of the exterior entrance doors to your house

** The window in the middle bedroom along the back of the house was not fully sealed &amp; had some frost buildup. This can be corrected once the outside temps are warm enough to obtain a good closure.

Savings: There should be a minimum 14% reduction in overall air leakage achieved from completing the above air sealing that could potentially save $ 90 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 1.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 715 in rebates from the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative. 

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $715 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on achieving the target outlined above. This grant can increase up to $1,300 based on how much you exceed the target.

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.
</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral>
The cathedral ceiling of your house would appear to have R24 batt insulation. In conjunction with your extensive renovation plans, I have modeled the opportunity to strap the ceiling down another 1.5" and replace the existing insulation with R40 ......... either batt insulation or spray foam - whatever you choose.

Savings: By increasing the ceiling insulation from R24 up to a minimum R40, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the DHERP.

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors>
Your house has 2 exterior entrance doors and they all have some air leakage around them or along the bottom door sweep. You may be able to have new weather-stripping installed this summer, or perhaps you may choose to replace them with new ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units. If you replace them, I recommend doors with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 1.22 W/mÂ²K or an Energy Rating of a minimum 34.

Savings:
By replacing these 2 entry door systems with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 22 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 0.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $325.00 in rebates (2 door units @ $162.50 each) through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative.

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system (includes all windows in the door, sidelights and transom windows above) is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.</doors>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>
Your house currently has a 121 litre oil hot water tank. If you proceed with the furnace upgrade, the existing co-vented chimney should be eliminated for the hot water tank as well. I have modeled the opportunity to replace the hot water tank with a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher (eg: Toyotomi OM-122DW)

Savings: By replacing your current oil hot water tank with a direct-vent, oil-fired semi-on-demand hot water heater with an energy factor of 0.70 or higher, could potentially save $ 85 per year in energy costs, increase your annual energy usage by 1.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
 
To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
Your house is currently heated with an 83% efficient oil furnace, venting up a 5" chimney that is co-vented with the 6" chimney for the hot water tank. I did not see a combustion air supply pipe in the mechanical room to supply combustion air to either of these 2 appliances.

I have modeled the option to upgrade from this current furnace to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. The new furnace will no longer require the existing chimney &amp; can be direct vented out the front or side wall of the house, using 2 x PVC pipes.

Savings:
By upgrading to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency condensing oil furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE (including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat), you could potentially save $ 1,008 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.2 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Under the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $3,500 is available for replacing an older oil fired furnace with an ENERGY STARÂ® certified oil furnace of the 95% efficiency that I modeled. To determine eligibility, confirm that the unit is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.
</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>
Renewable Energy Systems

The Arctic Energy Alliance administers the Alternative Energy Technologies Program (AETP) that includes rebates for Solar PV systems, including integrated battery energy storage for NWT residents who are not connected to the hydro-electric grid. 

Savings: As a starting point for your further investigation, I modeled a 4 kW PV system, flush mounted on the front facing roof (East), directly connected to the Fort McPherson grid (ie: not including a battery storage system). This is estimated to produce about 3,226 kWh annually, reducing your electricity bill by $ 1,017, and qualifying for up to $20,000 from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program.

For more information and pre-approval rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
https://aea.nt.ca/program/renewable-energy/

NOTE: The GNWT Alternative Energy Technology Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

Canada Greener Homes Grant 

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1,300 per KW is available for installing a solar photovoltaic system technology that converts sunlight directly into electricity. If you choose to install a photovoltaic (PV) system, it is strongly recommended that a full assessment by a professional solar photovoltaic installer, including the measuring of solar irradiance (how much sunlight falls on the roof) is undertaken to provide detailed information on considerations for your home and specific installation recommendations, including the size and related energy production of the system. The rated PV panel must have peak power capacity higher than or equal to 1.0 kW. The system must be composed of photovoltaic (PV) panel and inverter certified to CSA Standards. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.

Batteries Connected to Photovoltaic Systems to Provide Standby Power for Home

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative for Northern Arctic &amp; Off-Grid Communities, a grant of $1300 is available for the installation of battery storage and an inverter to connect to a photovoltaic system to provide standby power for the home for an existing PV system or a new install. This resiliency measure must be combined with an energy efficiency retrofit from the Canada Greener Homes Grant in order to be eligible. This grant may be combined with the new PV system installation grant for a maximum grant of $5000. Please see the Additional Comments section of this report for further information.
</Renewables>
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>
CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources. Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) and the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:
https://aea.nt.ca/program/home-improvements/
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: These programs are regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Wood Stove Heat - A new wood stove with heat circulated by your furnace fan !!

The Arctic Energy Alliance assigns a nominal cost of $100/cord for self-harvested wood (amortized purchase &amp; use of a chainsaw + the safety gear and transportation costs). I ran a quick calculation to see how much you would save (compared to using a new 95% efficient oil furnace) if you used a new 82.5% efficient wood stove (with a dedicated &amp; directly sealed outside combustion air supply) for 50% of the annual heating of your home.

I also mentioned an opportunity for you to have your heating contractor set up your thermostat so the furnace circulation fan could operate based upon a programmed "upper temp" setting to recirculate the heat from your woodstove down through the floor of your house &amp; all the way out to the bedrooms and bathroom along the back &amp; left walls. This would keep a more even heat in your house and help protect water and drain lines from freezing !!

The annual net heating cost savings (above &amp; beyond the estimated savings from the new furnace upgrade modeled previously in this report) is estimated to be $ 2,853 per year.

A qualified wood stove is eligible for 50% of purchase cost up to $ 1,000 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.
 
For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Fort McPherson, LED light bulbs and tubes are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from t he GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood &amp; clothes dryer - the bathroom fan was not working at the time of my visit), indicated a pressure drop of 2.7 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current furnace, hot water tank and wood stove are all chimney vented, without dedicated &amp; sealed combustion air supplies, so they are all potential risks for having combustion products back-drafted into the house.

During my assessment of your home, I saw one smoke detectors that appeared to be broken &amp; I did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following ventilation:
        - Kitchen range hood exhaust fan
        - 1 bathroom exhaust fan (was not working at the time of my visit)
        - Clothes dryer

If you choose to replace any exhaust fans, please select ENERGY STARÂ® qualified units. For the new bathroom fans, you should consider installing timer switches (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so they will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs - Fort McPherson

The following Nov. 1, 2022 values, before GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort McPherson:
Electricity - $18.00 min, $0.31 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.69 per kWh thereafter
Oil  - $2.73 per litre 
Wood - $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $14.00 per bag (picked up in Inuvik), $750.00 per tonne bulk delivered

